cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this kernel, cite OpenVDB, whose spline sampler it re-states.
title: fit.elf SDF spline kernel (Lean-emitted tricubic B-spline value, gradient and Hessian)
abstract: >-
  lean/Fit/SdfSplineHessian.lean re-states the tricubic uniform B-spline
  sampler of the Huangzizhou/openvdb fork (c96cb069 "spline hessian",
  openvdb/tools/Interpolation.h: SplineSampler::spline, spline_1st_deriv,
  spline_2nd_deriv and trilinearInterpolationHessian, behind
  SplineSampler::sampleHessian) as a LeanSlang compute kernel: value,
  gradient and symmetric Hessian over the 4^3 stencil at offsets -1..2, one
  thread per sample. lake exe emit_fit writes it as Slang into slang/, and
  gen.sh compiles it to cpp/ (the path fit.elf and fit_native compile,
  through vendor/cloth-fit/.../garment_forms/SdfSpline.cpp) and to SPIR-V
  (validation only; nothing embeds it yet). No OpenVDB source is copied;
  the arithmetic is followed operation for operation (per-axis basis and
  derivative tables, then i, j, k ascending, acc + ((v*a)*b)*c), so the
  kernel rounds as the fork does: Gate 6a measures 0 ULP in all 13 outputs
  against sampleHessian on OpenVDB's own stencils at 21,015 and 78,300
  samples. Local adaptations: double storage buffers instead of OpenVDB
  tree access (the caller gathers the stencil from its brick grid, SdfGrid);
  the Hessian written as its upper triangle (6 values) instead of a Mat3;
  every constant a double cast (double(2) / double(3) for 2/3), because an
  unsuffixed Slang literal is float; a count parameter bounding the lanes.
type: software
authors:
  - name: Zizhou Huang
  - name: OpenVDB contributors (Academy Software Foundation)
repository-code: https://github.com/V-Sekai-fire/openvdb
